{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Doctor: mapping-config diagnostics with offline-always / live-drift-degrades portability\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe mapping seam (S1–S8) adds a hand-editable `[mapping]` config surface. Two failure classes\nneed surfacing before a reconcile silently drifts:\n\n- **Internally-invalid config** — already fail-closed via `MappingConfigError`: a non-integer\n  `hierarchy` / malformed block fail at LOAD; an out-of-vocabulary value and an\n  unmapped-non-skipped type fail in the `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers. Doctor should\n  surface these as **error** findings.\n- **Live drift** — internally valid but disagreeing with live Jira (a mapped status/type/link\n  Jira no longer exposes). Surface when Jira is reachable; degrade cleanly when not.\n\n`rebar doctor` is the existing diagnostic home, so extend it, not a new command. It must stay\nportable: it runs where the optional **`jira-datacenter`** extra is absent and no credentials\nare set. Offline checks always run; the live-drift check degrades to an `unavailable` finding,\nmirroring `rebar metrics`'s `unavailable` (`docs/user-guide.md`).\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a mapping-diagnostics function to `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py`, wired into `scan`. Mapping findings go in their **own list** (as for scan-dirty/lock reports), are rendered and folded into the exit code, and kept **out of** `run_repair`'s\niteration — so `doctor.scan()`'s blocking-edge finding contract (and its\n`tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py`) is unchanged.\n\n- **Offline checks (always run, stdlib-only, no Jira import):** first call\n  `mapping_config.load_mapping_config` inside `try/except MappingConfigError` — a load-time\n  failure (non-integer hierarchy, malformed block) becomes one **error** finding whose key is read from the\n  error message's `[mapping.<where>]:` prefix (the class carries no structured attribute), and\n  the per-key pass is skipped. On a successful load, enumerate `config.projects` **union the default layer** and, per\n  key, run the provider-neutral `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers — `effective_status_map`,\n  `effective_type_map`, `assert_type_decisions_complete`, `effective_link_map` — which inject the\n  built-in `MappingLayer` (`local_to_jira_status` / `LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA` / link literal) +\n  `Capability`, import `mapping_config` lazily, and are stdlib-only at import (never\n  `adapters.jira*`); a resolve-time `MappingConfigError` (out-of-vocabulary value,\n  unmapped-non-skipped type) → an **error** finding naming the key (non-zero exit). On the same\n  per-key pass, report one genuinely *soft* problem that is NOT a load error — an all-empty\n  `projects.<KEY>` block (likely stub) — as a **warning** naming the key.\n- **Live-drift check (best-effort, degrades):** run only when the `jira-datacenter` extra is\n  importable — gated through `rebar._optional.capability_installed(\"jira_datacenter\")` — AND\n  credentials resolve. On the available path, reuse S8's read-only probe reader\n  (`mapping_probe.build_probe`) and diff configured vs observed Jira vocabulary, one finding per\n  divergence on the axes the reader observes: **status, type, link** (a configured value absent\n  from Jira's set).\n  Hierarchy is deliberately **excluded** from the live diff: `build_probe` resolves Data Center,\n  where `mapping_probe._hierarchy` is always empty (`hierarchyLevel` is Cloud-only) and\n  `hierarchy` is dropped — so a hierarchy divergence is structurally undetectable via this reader\n  (offline hierarchy validity is still covered above). When the\n  extra is absent, OR the resolved PAT is empty (`JIRA_PAT` unset — `resolve_jira_datacenter_settings`\n  is total, returning `pat=\"\"`), OR settings resolution raises `ConfigError` (fail-loud on a\n  malformed `[tool.rebar.reconciler]`), OR the probe\n  transport raises or times out (the probe call runs under an explicit doctor-side bounded\n  wall-clock timeout — a daemon worker thread whose `result(timeout=…)` bound is a\n  module constant (default 10s), since S8's `build_client_from_settings` sets no client timeout;\n  a timed-out thread is abandoned and doctor returns promptly with `unavailable` — the host never\n  blocks doctor), emit a single `unavailable` finding\n  (\"live mapping-drift check skipped: <reason>\") and return success — never raise. The Jira import and settings/probe construction\n  happen lazily inside the check, behind the guard and wrapped so every degradation cause folds\n  into the one `unavailable` finding.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py` — new mapping-diagnostics function (own findings list); the\n  `capability_installed(\"jira_datacenter\")` guard and lazy Jira import.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` — document the offline error/warning findings and the `unavailable`\n  live-drift convention.\n- Reuses S1's `mapping_config` (via `config` resolvers) and S8's `mapping_probe.build_probe`.\n- Out of scope: `--repair` auto-fixing mapping config (report-only); Jira as a hard doctor\n  dependency; the live-drift **true path against real Jira** — E2E transport is S8's proven\n  read-only probe reader (covered by S8), so this child proves the diff logic via an injected\n  vocabulary and treats the real-transport path as not-applicable.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Unit (offline tier), no Jira import:** temp config with (a) valid mapping → no findings;\n  (b) each error-class problem — a load-time one (non-integer hierarchy) and a resolve-time one\n  (out-of-vocabulary value, unmapped-non-skipped type) → an **error** finding naming the key +\n  non-zero exit; (c) an all-empty `projects.<KEY>` block → a **warning**. Asserts\n  no `adapters.jira` import on this path. Oracle: finding list + exit code.\n- **Unit (portability tier):** force the guard false (monkeypatch `capability_installed` for\n  `\"jira_datacenter\"`) → exactly one `unavailable` finding, success exit, no Jira import. Oracle:\n  the single finding + zero exit.\n- **Unit (degradation causes):** guard true; drive each cause — empty resolved PAT, a\n  `ConfigError` from settings resolution, and a slow/hanging probe reader exceeding the\n  doctor-side timeout — each folds into one `unavailable` finding with the matching reason +\n  success exit. Oracle: the finding + reason + zero exit.\n- **Unit (live-drift tier), injected transport:** inject a fake probe reader whose vocabulary\n  diverges on status, type, and link → one drift finding per divergence naming the axis and\n  absent value. No live Jira. Oracle: the per-axis finding set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar doctor` on a valid `[mapping]` config with the `jira-datacenter` extra absent\n  succeeds and emits exactly one `unavailable` live-drift finding — portability-tier test (guard\n  forced false via `capability_installed`, no Jira import, zero exit).\n- [ ] Each offline error-class problem (non-integer hierarchy, out-of-vocabulary value,\n  unmapped-non-skipped type) yields an **error** finding naming the key + non-zero exit — the\n  offline check enumerates `config.projects` + the default layer per key; offline-tier test\n  running the `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers and asserting the\n  `MappingConfigError`-derived finding.\n- [ ] An all-empty `projects.<KEY>` stub yields a **warning** finding naming the key (not an\n  error, not a non-zero exit) — offline-tier test.\n- [ ] Each degradation cause — extra absent, empty resolved PAT (`JIRA_PAT` unset), a\n  `ConfigError` from settings resolution, and a slow/hanging probe exceeding the doctor-side\n  timeout — folds into a single `unavailable` finding + reason + success exit — portability and\n  degradation-cause tests.\n- [ ] With a fake probe reader injected, each configured-vs-observed divergence on status, type,\n  and link yields a drift finding naming the axis and absent value — live-drift-tier test (hierarchy is excluded by design: the reused Data Center probe never\n  observes `hierarchyLevel`).\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md` documents the new mapping diagnostics.\n\nAll four tiers run in-process, no live Jira, no specific CI provider — satisfying\n`project.portability`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787392431876739001,"uuid":"f7dda585-c449-44c7-9ea6-3b157daa06e7"}